    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>Nasal blockage, thick discolored nasal drainage, pressure over the left cheek and forehead, and a reduced sense of smell for about five months, with pressure pain in the left cheek on descent when flying. Anterior rhinoscopy shows swollen, red inferior turbinates without visible polyps or pus; SNOT-22 52. Suspected chronic rhinosinusitis; nasal endoscopy and otolaryngology consultation booked</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>R09.81</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting problem linked to her documented chronic sinus trouble; superseded by the diagnosis at the consultation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps: more than 12 weeks of nasal blockage, discolored drainage, facial pressure and reduced smell, confirmed by nasal endoscopy showing mucosal swelling and mucopurulent drainage from the left middle meatus, with no polyps on either side; an acute exacerbation at diagnosis. Synthetic modern episode linked to her documented chronic sinus trouble</td><td>active</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>J32.9</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made at the otolaryngology consultation.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Left middle ear barotrauma after an airline flight taken during a cold: ear pain, fullness and muffled hearing from the descent, with a retracted left eardrum, a little bleeding within the drum, and fluid behind it; conductive hearing loss on tuning-fork tests; no vertigo or signs of inner-ear injury; resolved</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>T70.0XXA</td><td>S — Synthetic acute injury; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

