<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps (J32.9)</content>. Good result five and a half weeks after surgery: SNOT-22 12, from 41 before surgery and 52 at the first visit, a change well beyond the smallest important difference; facial pain 1 of 10; smell returning; healing well at the three-week cleaning. Chronic rhinosinusitis is a long-term condition, and continued daily topical treatment lowers the chance of recurrence. Flying again with no pressure pain. She should report the diagnosis, treatment and surgery to her aviation medical examiner at her next medical examination. Influenza vaccine given today; she declined the COVID-19 vaccine for now. [S]<list><item>Continue the saline and budesonide rinse every morning and plain saline at night.</item><item>Otolaryngology review with endoscopy three months after surgery.</item><item>Return early for blockage with discolored drainage, facial pain, or pressure pain on descent.</item><item>Never fly while blocked; use only medicines acceptable for pilots, with a 48-hour ground trial of anything new.</item><item>Review here in six months.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>SNOT-22 total score (0 to 110; 20 or more after medical therapy supports surgery)</td><td>At diagnosis, after each treatment step, and after surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>12, from 41 before surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Facial pain or pressure score (0 to 10)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>1 of 10, from 4</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Nasal endoscopy (polyps, edema, pus, crusting, healing)</td><td>To confirm the diagnosis, at flares, and after surgery</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Endoscopy with cleaning at one and three weeks after surgery: cavities open and healing; next three months after surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Warning signs of complications (eye swelling, vision change, severe headache, stiff neck, confusion)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None after surgery: no eye swelling, vision change, or clear watery drainage</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Surgical pathology</td><td>After surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Chronic inflammation with few eosinophils; no fungus; no tumor</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>After surgery: cavity cleaning, steroid rinse, and return to flying</td><td>One and three weeks after surgery, then three months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two cleanings done; budesonide rinse daily; cleared to fly at three weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Saline irrigation: daily use, technique, and safe water</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Twice daily; budesonide added in the morning</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Intranasal steroid: daily use, technique, nosebleeds</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Budesonide rinse in place of the spray; no nosebleeds</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicines acceptable for pilots and the 48-hour ground trial of a new medicine</td><td>With every new medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Budesonide rinse and acetaminophen acceptable; acetaminophen stopped; no sedating antihistamine</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Fitness to fly: not flying while blocked or in pain (14 CFR 61.53); sinus and ear clearing on descent</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Four flights without pressure pain; will report the surgery at her next aviation medical examination</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Endoscopic sinus surgery evaluation (appropriateness criteria and shared decision)</td><td>When symptoms persist after appropriate medical therapy with disease on CT</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Surgery done five and a half weeks ago; otolaryngology follow-up at three months</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>SNOT-22 total score below 20 by four months. Outcome by 1923-10-12: SNOT-22 12 five weeks after surgery [S]</item><item>Fly without sinus or ear pressure pain, and only when she meets the FAA rule of not flying while symptomatic, by four months. Outcome by 1923-10-12: Flying again since the ear, nose and throat clearance three weeks after surgery, without pressure pain [S]</item><item>Use only medicines acceptable for pilots, with a 48-hour ground trial of each new medicine and no sedating antihistamine. Outcome by 1923-10-12: No-go medicines stopped at intake; each new medicine started on a non-flying day [S]</item><item>Saline irrigation and the nasal steroid used daily with correct technique. Outcome by 1923-08-17: Daily use with correct technique at ten weeks; no nosebleeds [S]</item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Influenza vaccine, 1923-1924 season [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis (2015); International consensus statement on allergy and rhinology: rhinosinusitis 2021 (2021); Defining appropriateness criteria for endoscopic sinus surgery during management of uncomplicated adult chronic rhinosinusitis: a RAND/UCLA appropriateness study (2016); Guide for Aviation Medical Examiners: Over-the-Counter (OTC) Medications Reference Guide, What OTC Medications Can I Take and Still Be Safe to Fly? (2024); Guide for Aviation Medical Examiners: Allergy - Antihistamine and Immunotherapy Medication (2026); Adult Immunization Schedule by Medical Condition and Other Indication (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Otolaryngology review three months after surgery. Review here in six months, or sooner if symptoms return. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
